{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"James Albert Michener ( ; February 3, 1907 – October 16, 1997) was an American author of more than 40 books, most of which were fictional, lengthy family sagas covering the lives of many generations in particular geographic locales and incorporating solid history.","title":"James A. Michener"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Michener was known for the popularity of his works; he had numerous bestsellers and works selected for Book of the Month Club.","title":"James A. Michener"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He was also known for his meticulous research behind the books.","title":"James A. Michener"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Why Bother?"","title":"Why Bother? (essay)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":", originally published as "Perchance to Dream: In the Age of Images, a Reason to Write Novels", is a literary essay by American novelist Jonathan Franzen.","title":"Why Bother? (essay)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It is often referred to as "The Harper's Essay".","title":"Why Bother? (essay)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" First published in the April 1996 issue of "Harper's" magazine, the essay concerns the persistence of reading within the context of technological growth and distraction.","title":"Why Bother? (essay)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Franzen recounts his meditations on the state and possibility of the novel form, often against the backdrop of his personal experience, eventually concluding that the novel still has potential cultural agency in the United States, and often gains it by paradoxical drives of both culture and author.","title":"Why Bother? (essay)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Jonathan Earl Franzen (born August 17, 1959) is an American novelist and essayist.","title":"Jonathan Franzen"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" His 2001 novel "The Corrections", a sprawling, satirical family drama, drew widespread critical acclaim, earned Franzen a National Book Award, was a Pulitzer Prize for Fiction finalist, earned a James Tait Black Memorial Prize and was shortlisted for the International IMPAC Dublin Literary Award.","title":"Jonathan Franzen"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" His novel "Freedom" (2010) garnered similar praise and led to an appearance on the cover of "Time" magazine alongside the headline "Great American Novelist".","title":"Jonathan Franzen"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Corrections is a 2001 novel by American author Jonathan Franzen.","title":"The Corrections"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It revolves around the troubles of an elderly Midwestern couple and their three adult children, tracing their lives from the mid-20th century to "one last Christmas" together near the turn of the millennium.","title":"The Corrections"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The novel was awarded the National Book Award in 2001 and the James Tait Black Memorial Prize in 2002.","title":"The Corrections"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Freedom is a 2010 novel by American author Jonathan Franzen.","title":"Freedom (Franzen novel)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was published by Farrar, Straus and Giroux.","title":"Freedom (Franzen novel)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "Freedom" received general acclaim from book critics, and was ranked one of the best books of 2010 by several publications, and has been described as a "Great American Novel".","title":"Freedom (Franzen novel)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Purity is a novel by American author Jonathan Franzen.","title":"Purity (novel)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" His fifth novel, it was published on September 1, 2015, by Farrar, Straus and Giroux.","title":"Purity (novel)"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: American
